  "final_text": "I built the dashboard and created the alert rule. Both exist in Kibana, and the rule is turned on.\n\n**1. What the data holds:** Each record in `kibana_sample_data_logs` is one web request, timestamped on the `timestamp` field. The main fields are:\n- **Request:** `url`, `request`, `extension`, `agent`, `referer`\n- **Result:** `response` (the HTTP status code, stored as text rather than a number) and `bytes`\n- **Who and where:** `clientip`, `geo.src`, `geo.dest`, `geo.coordinates`\n- **Client machine:** `machine.os`, `machine.ram`, `memory`\n\n**2. Dashboard \"EFF-7a89fadb Web logs overview\"** (http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-7a89fadb-web-logs-overview-f25fe43c27a2) has 7 panels:\n- Single-number panels for total requests, unique visitors (distinct client IPs) and total bytes served\n- A bar chart of requests over time, split by response code\n- A pie chart of response codes\n- A table of the top 10 URLs with request count and bytes\n- A bar chart of the top 10 source countries\n\n**3. Alert rule \"EFF-7a89fadb 5xx spike\"** (id `1b7e3852-1ff6-4610-87e0-dc3c1f87d375`):\n- It runs every minute on `kibana_sample_data_logs`.\n- It counts requests with a response code from 500 to 599 over the last 5 minutes and fires when the count is more than 10.\n- I changed one setting after creating it. By default, each run skipped requests an earlier run had already counted. Because the 5-minute windows overlap, the rule would only have counted the newest minute's errors and could miss a real spike. Each run now counts every 5xx request in its full window.\n- It has no notification actions attached, so when it fires, the alert only appears in Kibana.\n\nI couldn't run a test query against the data directly, because the route I tried is turned off on this Kibana. So I haven't confirmed how many 5xx requests the rule's filter matches.",
